# Feature plans and GitHub issues

Use this when a distinct plan will change a feature or other functionality in the podverse
repo (`podverse/podverse`). The link is how we know which open issue gets a comment and a
close after that work is on `develop`.

Skip plans that do not change functionality: agent guidance, dependency bumps, and refactors
that leave behavior the same. A pasted issue (**abcissue**) is the issue for that work. Do
not open a second one.

## Search before the plan and again before implementation

Before writing the plan, search open issues (`gh issue list`, then `gh issue view` on each
match). Search again before the first product edit of implementation. Read the matches.

One feature uses one issue, including a set of several plans for that feature. Features that
could ship separately each get their own issue. Record every issue this plan will address
under `## Issue` on the plan (URL and number). When the set has `00-REQUEST.md`, record it
there too.

## Create the issue without waiting for plan approval

When no open issue covers the work, create one with `gh issue create`. Follow **github**
§ Creating and updating issues for the title and body. Read enough of the code that those
claims are true.

Create it as the first part of doing the plan. In the turn that writes the plan, file it once
the behavior to name is clear. When a saved plan has no issue, file it as the first
implementation step, before product edits. Operator approval of the plan is not a gate. File
the issue, then keep going.

Open product questions stay questions. File once the behavior the issue should name is clear.

`gh issue create` is the only GitHub write this rule allows. Comment, close, and edit stay
with the operator. See **operator-only-git-operations**.

## Recommend the comment and the close only after develop

Name the issue in the implementation summary. A drafted reply may be saved with the plan
(**abcissue** § Ticket reply). Saving that draft is not a prompt to post it.

After the change is merged into `develop`, recommend that the operator post a short comment
and close the issue. Give them the comment text. A few sentences on what the reporter can do
now. No file lists or plan names. Do not run `gh issue comment` or `gh issue close`.
